Template: fiveos/intro
Type: note
Description: FiveOS - FiveM server setup
 The next questions configure your FiveM server, MariaDB and phpMyAdmin.
 Everything else is installed and configured automatically.
 .
 On the first boot FiveOS downloads the current recommended FXServer build
 and finishes the setup, so the machine needs an internet connection.
 .
 A FiveM license key can be created at https://portal.cfx.re. You can leave
 it empty now and add it later with: sudo fiveos config license <key>

Template: fiveos/error
Type: error
Description: Invalid input
 ${MESSAGE}

Template: fiveos/server_name
Type: string
Default: My FiveOS Server
Description: FiveM server name:
 The name shown in the FiveM server list (sv_hostname).

Template: fiveos/license_key
Type: string
Default:
Description: FiveM license key (optional):
 Your Cfx.re server license key (sv_licenseKey), e.g. cfxk_xxxxxxxx.
 Leave empty to add it later. The server will not start without it.

Template: fiveos/db_admin_user
Type: string
Default: admin
Description: Database admin user:
 A MariaDB user with full privileges. Use it to log in to phpMyAdmin.

Template: fiveos/db_admin_password
Type: password
Description: Password for the database admin user:
 At least 8 characters.

Template: fiveos/db_admin_password_again
Type: password
Description: Repeat the password for the database admin user:

Template: fiveos/db_name
Type: string
Default: fivem
Description: Name of the FiveM database:
 The database your server scripts (ESX, QBCore, oxmysql, ...) use.

Template: fiveos/db_user
Type: string
Default: fivem
Description: Database user for the FiveM server:
 This user only has access to the FiveM database. FiveOS writes the matching
 mysql_connection_string into the server configuration.

Template: fiveos/db_password
Type: password
Description: Password for the FiveM database user:
 At least 8 characters.

Template: fiveos/db_password_again
Type: password
Description: Repeat the password for the FiveM database user:
